WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
A free ai chat that needs zero sign up just appeared and it works right in your browser. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 145. DuckDuckGo just launched Luna, a free ai chat that opens straight in your browser with nothing to create or remember. You type a question and it answers right away, then forget everything the moment you close the tab. Think of it like borrowing a notebook from the library that erases itself after you finish reading.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:55.960
Most ai chats ask for an email or phone number first, but Luna skips that step completely, so students and curious beginners can jump in during a study break. The tool uses the same kind of language model that powers other chats, yet it keeps every conversation private by default and deletes the history automatically. That matters because it removes the usual worry that old questions might shape future answers or get saved somewhere.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:19.900
For you, it means you can ask about photosynthesis, brainstorm a story idea, or check a quick fact without extra accounts or permission hurdles. Open your browser, head to the DuckDuckGo site, look for the ai chat option in the search bar, and type something like explain how a battery works in simple terms. Keep chatting as long as you like and close the tab when you are done.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:49.880
The service positions itself as a quick utility rather than a long term commitment, showing how ai tools are becoming easier to reach for everyday people. Here is the part that sets this show apart from quick news updates. We are going to walk through exactly how these chats build their answers, one piece at a time, using an everyday habit you already know. You know how your phone sometimes guesses the next word while you are texting a friend? Now stretch that same idea across an entire background.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:02:16.200
Back-and-forth conversation instead of one short sentence. The ai looks at every word you have typed so far, checks patterns it learned from billions of other sentences, and picks what feels like the most natural next piece. It repeats the process over and over, building each new word based on everything that came before in the chat. At the same time, it keeps track of the overall topic so the answers stay on track instead of drifting into random territory.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:35.220
When the model sees the word photosynthesis, it does not just recall a definition. It pulls the most likely follow-up words that match how people usually explain the process in textbooks or videos. Each new sentence is shaped by the growing context so the explanation stays consistent from start to finish.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:51.340
The simple loop of look at what came before, predict what comes next, is the core of how these tools create their replies. That step-by-step prediction is why the answers often feel smooth and connected, even though the system is only choosing one piece at a time.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:03:00.740
Once you see the pattern, the whole process stops feeling mysterious and starts feeling like a very fast, very well-read autocomplete that remembers the thread.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:17.360
Chat GPT now lets you upload a photo or describe an idea and instantly turn it into a sticker you can save and use in messages. It works by taking your input and generating a small transparent image that feels like a fun emoji version of whatever you described.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:26.340
Go to the chat GPT site on your phone or laptop, start a new chat and type something like make a sticker of my dog wearing sunglasses.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:35.060
Upload a photo if you prefer, ask it to turn the subject into a sticker, then download the result and add it to your messaging app collection.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:41.140
The feature turns everyday snapshots into shareable graphics without needing extra design software.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:48.920
Chat GPT and Gemini both have camera features that let you point your phone at real objects and ask questions about them.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:57.920
One might notice small details faster while the other gives clearer explanations, so testing them side-by-side shows which one feels more useful.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:06.460
Open both apps, point the camera at something nearby like a snack package or a house plant, and ask the same question in each one.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:12.520
Notice which one gives more helpful details for your needs, whether you are shopping or just exploring.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:19.640
The side-by-side test reveals practical differences in how each system processes visual information in real time.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:28.720
A simple setting in most chatbot apps lets you turn off data sharing so your conversations stay private and are not used to improve the system later.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.920
It takes less than a minute and gives you more control over what information stays yours.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:40.640
Check the option in the account or privacy menu to prevent your questions from becoming part of future training data.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:47.540
Alibaba released a new video tool that turns text descriptions or uploaded PDFs into short clips.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:54.300
You can type an idea or share a document and receive a 30 second video that turns the content into moving visuals.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:05:02.920
The tool accepts text, PDFs, and presentation files as starting points, giving students a fast option for creating animated explanations

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:06.070
without learning video editing software first.

